		summary = "**Manta rays** are large [[Batomorphi|rays]] belonging to the [[Genus|genus]] Mobula (formerly its own [[Genus|genus]] Manta). Three species are known: M. birostris, the largest at 7 m in width, M. yarae, which reaches 6 m, and M. alfredi, the smallest at 5.5 m. All three have triangular pectoral fins, horn-shaped cephalic fins and large, forward-facing mouths. They are classified among the Myliobatiformes ([[Stingray|stingrays]] and relatives) and are placed in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] Myliobatidae (eagle rays). They have one of the highest brain-to-body ratio of all fish, advanced cognition, and may understand mirror contingencies.",
		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"Mantas are found in warm [[Temperate climate|temperate]], [[Subtropics|subtropical]] and [[Tropics|tropical]] waters. All three species are pelagic; M. birostris and M. yarae [[Animal migration|migrate]] across open oceans, singly or in groups, while M. alfredi tends to be resident and coastal. They are [[Filter feeder|filter feeders]] and eat large quantities of [[Zooplankton|zooplankton]], which they gather with their open mouths as they swim. However, research suggests that the majority of their diet comes from mesopelagic sources. [[Gestation]] lasts over a year and mantas give birth to live pups. Like whales, they breach for unknown reasons. M. birostris and M. alfredi are both listed as [[Vulnerable species|vulnerable]] by the [[International Union for Conservation of Nature]]. [[Human impact on the environment|Anthropogenic]] threats include [[Pollution|pollution]], entanglement in fishing nets, and direct harvesting of their gill rakers for use in Chinese medicine. Manta rays are particularly valued for their gill plates, which are traded internationally.",
